# Group Management and Shared Folder Permissions

## What I worked on

Today I continued building on my Active Directory environment, focusing on managing security groups and controlling access to shared folders.

### 1. Group management

I reviewed the security groups I'd previously created for IT, Finance and Sales.

I then practised nesting groups so that users can receive access to resources through their department groups, rather than assigning permissions to every individual user.

This helped me understand how organisations can manage access more easily as their number of users grows.

### 2. Creating shared folders

I created separate Sales and Finance folders on the server.

I worked through the folder security settings and configured permissions so that each department has its own access.

I also learned the difference between folder permissions and network sharing permissions, and why both need to be considered when setting up shared resources.

### 3. Testing access

I created network shares for Sales and Finance and checked that they were available through the server.

I started testing access using different user accounts.

I came across an issue where Sarah's account appeared to have access to the Finance folder when it shouldn't. I checked her group memberships and found no direct membership of the Finance groups.

I haven't fully resolved this yet, so I'll return to it in a future session.

### 4. PowerShell practice

I continued developing my PowerShell skills by practising ways to retrieve and manage information in Active Directory.

Today I worked on:

- Listing user accounts and their details.
- Checking which accounts are enabled or disabled.
- Exporting user information into a CSV report.
- Finding disabled user accounts.
- Looking at group membership and shared folder permissions.


## What I've learned

Today has helped me understand how security groups can be used to manage access to shared resources.

I've also gained more experience with Windows folder permissions and network sharing.

I'm becoming more comfortable using PowerShell to retrieve information from Active Directory 
